{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"a33e3d07d46d0b48","name":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Japan/Homefront/events/card_event_drive_into_the_sea","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"DRIVE INTO THE SEA","text":"Remove a unit from the battlefield. At the start of your next turn, return it to hand.","flavorText":"In early 1942, Japanese forces tightened their grip on the Philippines, forcing American and Filipino troops into a desperate defense on the island fortress of Corregidor. After months of aerial and naval bombardment, the final assault began on May 5th. Under cover of darkness and artillery fire, Japanese troops crossed Manila Bay and landed on Corregidor’s northeastern coast. Despite the defenders’ fierce resistance, the attackers pushed inland, overwhelming beach defenses and securing a critical foothold. US and Filipino units launched counterattacks through the night, but Japanese reinforcements steadily poured ashore. The defenders were low on ammunition, rations and medical supplies. Communications broke down. By the afternoon of May 6th, Japanese troops had breached the Malinta Tunnel area, the last major Allied stronghold. Facing total collapse, Lieutenant General Jonathan Wainwright surrendered all forces on Corregidor. The fall of the island marked the end of organized resistance in the Philippines. Of the roughly 11,000 Allied troops captured, many would face brutal conditions in captivity.","faction":"Japan","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"GameplayTags":["ability.bond"],"Name":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea","title":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea_title","SourceString":"DRIVE INTO THE SEA","LocalizedString":"DRIVE INTO THE SEA"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Japan","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea_text","SourceString":"Remove a unit from the battlefield. At the start of your next turn, return it to hand.","LocalizedString":"Remove a unit from the battlefield. At the start of your next turn, return it to hand."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea_history","SourceString":"In early 1942, Japanese forces tightened their grip on the Philippines, forcing American and Filipino troops into a desperate defense on the island fortress of Corregidor. After months of aerial and naval bombardment, the final assault began on May 5th. Under cover of darkness and artillery fire, Japanese troops crossed Manila Bay and landed on Corregidor’s northeastern coast. Despite the defenders’ fierce resistance, the attackers pushed inland, overwhelming beach defenses and securing a critical foothold. US and Filipino units launched counterattacks through the night, but Japanese reinforcements steadily poured ashore. The defenders were low on ammunition, rations and medical supplies. Communications broke down. By the afternoon of May 6th, Japanese troops had breached the Malinta Tunnel area, the last major Allied stronghold. Facing total collapse, Lieutenant General Jonathan Wainwright surrendered all forces on Corregidor. The fall of the island marked the end of organized resistance in the Philippines. Of the roughly 11,000 Allied troops captured, many would face brutal conditions in captivity.","LocalizedString":"In early 1942, Japanese forces tightened their grip on the Philippines, forcing American and Filipino troops into a desperate defense on the island fortress of Corregidor. After months of aerial and naval bombardment, the final assault began on May 5th. Under cover of darkness and artillery fire, Japanese troops crossed Manila Bay and landed on Corregidor’s northeastern coast. Despite the defenders’ fierce resistance, the attackers pushed inland, overwhelming beach defenses and securing a critical foothold. US and Filipino units launched counterattacks through the night, but Japanese reinforcements steadily poured ashore. The defenders were low on ammunition, rations and medical supplies. Communications broke down. By the afternoon of May 6th, Japanese troops had breached the Malinta Tunnel area, the last major Allied stronghold. Facing total collapse, Lieutenant General Jonathan Wainwright surrendered all forces on Corregidor. The fall of the island marked the end of organized resistance in the Philippines. Of the roughly 11,000 Allied troops captured, many would face brutal conditions in captivity."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Uncommon","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnStartofTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Taiko_Hits_2'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/War_UI/Waves/Drum_Movements/War_UI_Drum_Movement_Taiko_Hits_2.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Japan/10_Homefront/T_drive_into_the_sea.T_drive_into_the_sea","SubPathString":""},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/DRIVE_INTO_THE_SEA.DRIVE_INTO_THE_SEA","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":346.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_japan","Key":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea_photo","SourceString":"American and Filipino prisoners captured at Corregidor, 1942.","LocalizedString":"American and Filipino prisoners captured at Corregidor, 1942."},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/BP_AttackOrder.BP_AttackOrder_C","SubPathString":""},"isRemoval":true},"variables":[],"functions":[{"name":"CanPlayFromHand","owner":"card_event_drive_into_the_sea_C","params":[{"name":"canIt","type":"bool","direction":"out"},{"name":"reason","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam1","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"reasonParam2","type":"FString","direction":"out"},{"name":"targetedCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"out